e magic  of Ollo Canyon  Oh canyons incredibly beautiful and then  when the light changes the reflections  of the potholes just make you think that  you're you know seeing some kind of  divine intervention you should go into  these places it's just my favorite  places in Grand Canyon  I could have spent days studying the  secret to this labyrinth but our  stomachs won't allow it if we don't keep  moving  [Applause]  by early February we emerge from our  coldest hardest leg at Havasu Creek once  again a storm of controversy and debate  on the rim above us could play a hand in  the future of the natural Cathedral and  Springs we walked through mining in and  around the park has been a charged  subject for decades recently it has  ignited again with the reopening of the  Canyon Mine on the South Rim just  outside the park boundary they have a  suit by Native American tribe are some  of the longest inhabitants of Grand  Canyon and they are concerned that a  mining accident might change their world  this is the actual site of the canyon  mine my reservation is about twenty  miles from here and we feel that this  mind behind me is going to contaminate  our ground water source and when it  floods here it goes down to the super  village where 800 members of my tribe  live down there and when this  contamination does reach our home  there'll be no more have a seat Pike  mining that we do out here is heavily  regulated there are major controls for  water protection both ground and surface  water protection which basically means  that no water can enter or exit the site  except through precipitation and even  precipitation that falls on this site is  diverted to a operation pond and it's  evaporated the Spirit is very powerful  you see the rains that have come in to  the east lured there's flooding this bag  overflows and it goes down the ravine it  goes straight into our home and it will  leak into the ground and it will begin  to affect our people's water I'll tell  you from a water standpoint there's a  variety of reasons why we're not  concerned we're well above any sort of a  regional aquifer there's no known  connections from even the bottom of  these mines to the regional aquifers  groundwater is kind of a black box in  science water infiltrates into the earth  and flows in fractures and fault lines  and comes out someplace but really the  path of water through the ground is  quite mysterious and therefore the  trustworthiness of anybody's claim about  the flow directions about the timing of  the flow path the duration of the flow  path of that groundwater needs to be  reviewed everybody in the country wants  all of the positive thinks that energy  gives you your cell phone your computer  your TV your heat your lights everything  that we use runs off of power we didn't  put the uranium here this is the best  place in the United States where uranium  occurs but again with our small-scale  mining that we're doing here I just  don't think that there is any reason to  have any concern for the Grand Canyon  everybody's talking about money  everybody wants to make money our main  concern is our lives  we're talking about human lives here  once again Kevin and I leave a brewing  debate on the surface of the canyon to  tackle our own challenges we have  another 200 miles to go through western  Grand Canyon which some call the gods  scape  [Music]

